# Cartwright: Lares (Tasked Spirit)
## Infinite canvas for a DOM-cooperating, local-first wiki vessel — field report, 2026-09-02

**Method `!`** — the session's WebSearch budget was exhausted at call zero, so *nothing below comes from a search-engine summary*. Everything is primary: the authenticated GitHub REST API (exact `pushed_at`, commit timestamps, `stats/participation` weekly counts, raw LICENSE bytes read out of base64), npm registry, and the people's own sites and source. Where a thing could not be grounded I say **no source found** rather than fill it `0`.

---

## A. tldraw specifically

### Steve Ruiz has not moved on. He is merging today.

- GitHub profile: **"Founder & CEO at @tldraw"**, London. https://github.com/steveruizok
- He merged commits to `tldraw/tldraw` on **2026-08-27 through 2026-09-02**, and authored the **SDK 5.4 release post dated 2026-09-02** — today. https://github.com/tldraw/tldraw/commits/main · https://tldraw.dev/blog

### Who merges, and how fast

- **50,090★**, not archived, `pushed_at` 2026-09-02. **v5.4.0 published 2026-09-02**; v5.3 on 2026-08-05.
- Weekly commit counts, last 12 weeks: `63, 82, 18, 41, 25, 17, 19, 24, 52, 58, 149, 20`. No gap anywhere in 52 weeks. This is the healthiest-maintained project in the entire field.
- Mergers: **steveruizok, MitjaBezensek, kostyafarber, m31-galaxy, driev, max-drake**. A real staffed company.
- Funding: **$10M Series A, April 2025**; no 2026 raise announced. `0` — I found no 2026 funding post; absence of a post proves nothing about runway.
- Direction of travel worth noting: they shipped **"tldraw offline"** (2026-07-16, "the local whiteboard for you and your agents") and **offline multiplayer** (2026-08-13). tldraw is moving *toward* local-first — which makes the licence collision sharper, not softer.

### The licence, read verbatim — and it disqualifies us

I read `LICENSE.md` out of the API rather than trusting a summary. The operative clauses:

> "Not to use the Software in **Production Environments**." … "Production Environment" means any production deployment … "where the software is used to provide functionality to end users, customers, or the public."
>
> "Not to **disable, change, or interfere with** the Software's License Key enforcement."
>
> "Not to make the Software available under a license that **supersedes or negates** the effect of this License."
>
> "The Software includes technical measures to verify License Key validity, **detect deployment environments**, enforce usage restrictions based on license type, and **ensure proper watermark display**. The Software **may collect and transmit usage data to tldraw** for license compliance purposes."

— https://github.com/tldraw/tldraw/blob/main/LICENSE.md

And from `packages/editor/src/lib/license/Watermark.tsx`, in-source, verbatim:

> "The tldraw watermark is part of tldraw's license. It is shown for **unlicensed or 'licensed-with-watermark'** users. By using this library, you agree to preserve the watermark's behavior, keeping it **visible, unobscured, and unmodified**."

**`!` Three independent hard failures against our constraints, any one of which is fatal:**
1. **No production use at all without a key.** There is no free-with-watermark *right* — the watermark tier is a licensed tier. The base grant is dev-only.
2. **Phone-home telemetry for compliance.** Constraint 2 says no mandatory server, no cloud precondition. A licence that reserves the right to transmit usage data and to *detect deployment environments* is structurally incompatible with a sovereign vessel — it is the enclosure Orion Reed writes about, in clause form.
3. **Cannot fork sovereignly.** No relicensing, no removal of enforcement, plus automatic termination if you ever assert a patent or copyright claim against tldraw *or any user of the Software*.

Pricing is "value-based — talk to sales", no published figure, with a 100-day trial and a free *non-commercial hobby* licence. https://tldraw.dev/pricing

### Architecture — and here is the cruelty: it is exactly right

tldraw does **not** rasterise. `HTMLContainer` is literally a passthrough div:

```tsx
export function HTMLContainer({ children, className = '', ...rest }) {
  return <div {...rest} className={classNames('tl-html-container', className)}>{children}</div>
}
```
— `packages/editor/src/lib/components/HTMLContainer.tsx`

The camera is **one shared CSS transform per layer** (`getHtmlLayerTransform`), values clamped by `toDomPrecision` to 3 decimals, with a zoom-dependent sub-pixel offset so the 1px layers stay registered with their siblings. `.tl-html-layer` carries `contain: layout style size`. Shapes are real DOM; real inputs, real selection, real focus, real a11y tree. **This is the reference implementation of the category we need.**

**Store `?`** — its own signals-based `TLStore`, specified in `packages/store/SPEC.md`, with records scoped `document` / `session` / `presence`, `RecordsDiff` change-sets, and a `source: 'user' | 'remote'` distinction where remote changes arrive through **`mergeRemoteChanges`**. That is a genuine external-source-of-truth seam.

**Prior art, tldraw + Automerge — it exists, and it has rotted.** `pvh/automerge-tldraw` — Peter van Hardenberg's own binding, README: *"A React hook which binds a TLDraw instance to a dedicated Automerge document… based on example code by Steve Ruiz of TLDraw."* It also says: *"there are no tests… The presence API is very sketchy."* **Last pushed 2025-06-12** — 15 months, against a tldraw that has since gone 3.x → 5.4. `!` Assume it does not run. https://github.com/pvh/automerge-tldraw

**Verdict on tldraw:** the best rendering-family fit in the field, disqualified on licence, and the disqualification is not a technicality — it is the precise deployment shape the licence was drafted to prevent.

