## B. The people

### Orion Reed — the nakama read is correct, and richer than expected

**Where he actually is:** GitHub bio, verbatim — *"Seeking to catalyze discontent with the state of computing. **Researcher at @inkandswitch and @libcomp**"* (Liberatory Computing). His site adds: **¼ of Tentpole, ½ of folkjs, co-organiser of OCWG**, previously **engineer at websim and tldraw**. https://github.com/OrionReed · https://orionreed.com

`!` **He is ex-tldraw, currently Ink & Switch.** His most recent public GitHub activity is **2026-08-14 on `inkandswitch/gaios.sgai.uk`** and **2026-08-11 creating `inkandswitch/patchwork-system`**. The Ink & Switch ↔ Orion seam you suspected is not a seam — he is inside the lab, on the Patchwork line, which is the same Automerge substrate we already run.

**His positions, in his own words.** His essay *"Digital Topology & Economic Power"* (**2026-02-26**) is, to a first approximation, our project's thesis stated by someone else:

> "the *file* was a relatively autonomous object, existing separately from the application that produced it… For users, file-app separation was a source of agency: your work existed independently of the tools which operated on it, and switching costs stayed low."
>
> "**Enclosure does not need to abolish the file. It needs only to make the boundary opaque.**"
>
> "enclosure succeeds not by offering users *less*, but by **bundling genuine capability gains with topological restructuring**, so that the user cannot accept the improvement without accepting the new relationship of power."
>
> "**The topology has changed such that the *file* is no longer a sufficient vessel for the *work*.**"

`?` That last line is the argument *for* a CRDT-backed bag rather than a file, made from the political-economy side. It is also, read against section A, the exact indictment of tldraw's licence.

His other position piece, *"Scoped Propagators: A programming model for spatial canvases"* (2024-07-08), argues for putting computation **on edges rather than nodes** so that *"you do not need to know at design-time what node types exist"* — a way to add liveness to *"environments which were not designed with liveness in mind."* `?` For us that reads directly: a spatial layer over TW5 tiddlers whose types we do not control at design time.

**His code — folkjs.** https://github.com/folk-js/folkjs — **MIT, 227★.** Co-built with Chris Shank. Stated questions, from the README:

> "How easily can we turn a HTML document into a spatial canvas and vice versa? What would it look like if the **browser had primitives for building spatial canvases**? How can someone annotate, re-layout, and program web pages they did not make or distribute?"

I read the source. `FolkSpace` is a **plain Web Component** — `folk-space` — whose content div is a real DOM subtree under `transform-origin: 0 0`, with `MIN_SCALE = 0.05`, `MAX_SCALE = 8`. Category (ii), no ambiguity. `packages/geometry` carries the same `toDOMPrecision = Math.round(v * 1e4) / 1e4` clamp tldraw uses. `packages/collab/src/automerge.ts` **already exists** — folkjs speaks Automerge. And `ShiftingOriginGraph.ts` implements *infinite* zoom by **re-basing the reference frame** ("A graph that supports infinite zooming by changing reference frames"), which is the escape hatch past the float-precision wall that bounded `MAX_SCALE = 8`.

**`_` The hidden problem: both authors have left.** folkjs last commit **2026-05-19**; weekly participation shows **~16 consecutive zero weeks**. Orion's last folkjs commits (April 2026) were radiance-cascade and raytracing work — his attention moved to graphics research, then to Ink & Switch. Chris Shank has moved to **Semble** (ATProto social knowledge tool, `cosmik-network/semble`, active 2026-09-01) and a Zotero plugin `zemble`. https://github.com/ChrisShank

**Verdict:** folkjs is the most architecturally aligned artifact in existence for our problem, it is MIT, it already speaks Automerge — and it is a **cooling research prototype, never published to npm**, whose two authors both redirected in mid-2026. Read it, mine it, do not depend on it.

