## E. The hard problems, from people who shipped one

**Not abstract — these are measured constants and real issue numbers.**

**How many live DOM nodes before it dies.** tldraw — the most optimised DOM-hosting canvas that exists — ships these defaults in `packages/editor/src/lib/options.ts`:
- `maxShapesPerPage: 4000`, `maxPages: 40`
- `debouncedZoom: true` with **`debouncedZoomThreshold: 500`** — *above 500 shapes they stop reporting the live zoom level* and serve a cached one, to stop re-render storms.

`!` So: **~500 objects is where a shipped DOM canvas starts lying to itself, and ~4000 is the declared ceiling.**

**Virtualisation.** tldraw's culling docs (`sdk-features/culling.mdx`, updated 2025-12-29) — culled shapes **stay in the DOM with `display: none`**, deliberately, so they remain selectable, hit-testable and exportable. They are excluded from culling while selected or being edited. `_` But `display:none` **removes the node from the accessibility tree and drops focus** — which is precisely the constraint-1 cost. The 2026 answer is **`content-visibility: auto`** (Baseline 2024): it skips layout and paint off-screen **while keeping the element in the DOM and in the a11y tree**, paired with `contain-intrinsic-size`. `!` Sharp caveat: tldraw's own `editor.css` has `content-visibility: auto` **commented out** on `.tl-html-container` — they tried it and backed it out. Understand why before relying on it.

**Zoom and text.** The failure mode in a CSS-transform canvas is *not* focus or selection — those work, because the node is genuinely in the DOM. It is **caret and font-metric rendering when the app computes size by multiplying by scale in JS**:
- tldraw **#8377** (fixed): at ~800% zoom carets rendered wildly oversized because font-size was computed as `fontSize * scale`. Fix: compose a real `transform: scale()` on the label container instead.
- tldraw **#8379** (open): same bug recurring on arrow labels, because the fix was not applied uniformly. `!` **This bug class must be fixed per-widget, not once globally.**
- tldraw **#9733** (open): caret invisible on empty lines when zoomed out.

**The rule practitioners converged on:** apply zoom as a real `transform: scale()` on the *smallest wrapper around editable text*; never recompute font-size, padding, or caret dimensions in JS from a scale variable.

**Float precision at extreme zoom.** Two independent codebases converge on the same clamp — tldraw's `toDomPrecision` and folkjs's `toDOMPrecision` both round DOM values to fixed decimals, and both bound zoom (`tldraw zoomSteps: [0.05 … 8]`; `FolkSpace MIN_SCALE 0.05 / MAX_SCALE 8`). **The same number, 8, in both.** The escape past it is folkjs's `ShiftingOriginGraph` — *"supports infinite zooming by changing reference frames"*, re-basing the origin node as you descend, with node culling during traversal. Its own header says: *"This is a PoC, lots of optimisations are possible."* `0` I found **no citable 2025–26 engineering writeup** on transform-origin re-basing; the concern is evidenced circumstantially by those clamps, not by a published account.

**Pointer / touch / pen, and what mobile does differently.** From tldraw's tracker, a shipped product's scar tissue:
- **#10285 (open, 2026-08-20):** *"Safari pencil double-tap zoom suppression never runs because its touch listeners check `instanceof PointerEvent`"*
- #6888, #6907, #7777: Safari pinch-zoom broken / resetting selection to previous shapes (recurring across 2025-10 → 2026-01)
- **#6911: `position: sticky` at the bottom does not work on Safari** — `!` sticky positioning and scroll containers under transform are a documented casualty
- **#8970:** cross-browser drift — strokes misalign with text between Chrome and Safari in multiplayer, *worsening per line*
- **#5539:** IME Enter key causes text duplication in Chrome — `!` IME does break, and it breaks in the composition/commit path, not the transform path
- **iOS Safari auto-zooms the page viewport when an `<input>` with `font-size ≤ 15px` takes focus.** Set 16px minimum on every focusable input. `0` That page-zoom compounds with the canvas transform; no source addresses the interaction — test it.

**`?` The honest summary of section E:** in a category-(ii) canvas, focus, selection, inputs and the a11y tree *survive*, because the nodes are really there. What breaks is (a) anything that computes pixels from a scale variable in JS, (b) `position: sticky` and scroll containment, (c) Safari's gesture layer, and (d) your object budget somewhere between 500 and 4000. Every one of those is a bounded engineering problem. None of them is the *unbounded* problem — rebuilding text editing and accessibility from scratch — that the rasteriser family hands you.

**CSS platform, 2026.** `zoom` is Baseline since May 2024 (Firefox 126 was the holdout, 96.6% global). It did **not** change the calculus: `zoom` **affects layout**, so a 60fps camera over thousands of nodes means layout thrash every frame, whereas `transform: scale()` does not. Its upside — crisp glyphs, because text is re-laid-out rather than pixel-scaled — is real and suits **discrete zoom steps** (which is exactly what tldraw's `zoomSteps` array is). Two tools, two jobs. `container-type` is orthogonal.

---

## THE FIVE WORTH OUR TIME

### 1. **xyflow** (React Flow / Svelte Flow) — the only library that clears both bars
- **What:** a node-graph library whose nodes are real React/Svelte components positioned by CSS transform. Live `<input>`, contenteditable, selection, focus, a11y tree — all real, because the node is in the DOM. Ships `nodrag`/`nowheel`/`nopan` classes precisely so gestures don't hijack interactive children; the seam exists because hosting live inputs is a first-class use case.
- **Why it survives:** category (ii) `✓`. And the store model is the decisive fit — the public API is a **controlled component**: you pass `nodes`/`edges` as props and receive `onNodesChange`/`onEdgesChange` as *proposed diffs*. You intercept, apply to Automerge, feed the array back. **xyflow does not own node state.** That is exactly the shape our composite store needs.
- **Licence:** **MIT**, webkid GmbH. No BSL, no CLA trap found. The "React Flow" badge in the free build is a **runtime UI badge, not a licence obligation** — MIT compels no attribution-in-UI, and `proOptions.hideAttribution` exists. Pro ($169–289/mo) sells removal as a convenience. `0` Have counsel confirm our reading; the text itself is plain MIT.
- **Aliveness:** `@xyflow/react` **12.11.6, 2026-09-01**. Daily merges by Klack and Kogo. Self-funded Berlin team, no VC.
- **The one thing that would disqualify it:** it is a *graph* library used as a general spatial substrate. If tiddler-objects need behaviours xyflow reserves for its own node lifecycle, the impedance mismatch stops being cosmetic.

### 2. **Build the substrate ourselves**, on `@panzoom/panzoom` or `d3-zoom`, following tldraw's *pattern*
- **What:** a `folk-space`-shaped custom element of our own — one shared CSS transform per layer, `toDomPrecision` clamping, `content-visibility: auto` for culling, ShiftingOriginGraph-style origin re-basing when we outgrow scale 8.
- **Why it survives:** zero licence exposure, zero store conflict (a camera controller owns nothing), and the DreamDOM work means we are already writing the VM-side layer that would drive it. **The `pattern` is not encumbered even though tldraw's code is** — reading `getHtmlLayerTransform` and reimplementing costs us nothing legally.
- **Licence:** MIT / ISC on the substrate; ours on the rest.
- **Aliveness:** `@panzoom/panzoom` 4.6.2 pushed **today**; `react-zoom-pan-pinch` 4.1.1 shipped **2026-09-02**; d3-zoom feature-complete under the d3 org.
- **Disqualifier:** we would own every one of section E's bugs ourselves — the caret-scale class, Safari's gesture layer, the 500-object cliff — with no upstream to inherit fixes from.

### 3. **folkjs** — as a quarry, not a dependency
- **What:** Orion Reed and Chris Shank's spatial-DOM research. `FolkSpace` (CSS-transform DOM canvas), `ShiftingOriginGraph` (infinite zoom by reference-frame shifting), `packages/collab/src/automerge.ts` (**already Automerge**), scoped propagators, `folk-shape-attribute` / `folk-space-attribute` as *custom attributes* rather than wrapper components.
- **Why it survives:** it is the only artifact in the world whose stated research questions are verbatim ours — *"How easily can we turn a HTML document into a spatial canvas?"* — written by a person the operator names nakama, already speaking our CRDT.
- **Licence:** **MIT.** Cleanest on the list.
- **Aliveness:** `!` **poor. Last commit 2026-05-19; ~16 zero weeks; never published to npm; README self-describes as research phase.** Orion → Ink & Switch; Chris → Semble.
- **Disqualifier:** already fired — it cannot be a dependency. It can be the best-quality source material we will find, and a live line to its author.

### 4. **OCIF (Open Canvas Interchange Format)** — the interop seat, not a renderer
- **What:** the extension-based interchange format for infinite-canvas tools. v0.7.0 Candidate Recommendation, March 2026.
- **Why it survives:** it costs a serialisation adapter and buys standing with Orion Reed, Jess Martin, Max Völkel and the Godot people. `?` Against Orion's own topology argument, an open interchange format *is* the anti-enclosure move — it is how the boundary stays legible. Add **JSON Canvas** import/export beside it as a cheap, 7-app, MIT-licensed courtesy.
- **Licence:** spec text **CC BY-SA 4.0**. `!` The repo carries **no LICENSE file** — read that before we vendor any schema.
- **Aliveness:** governance real (42 meetings), but repo quiet since 2026-04-10. `0` Settled CR or stall — undetermined.
- **Disqualifier:** if OCIF cannot carry a tiddler — arbitrary typed content with our own transclusion semantics — it becomes an export format only, not an interop surface.

### 5. **tldraw** — listed last so the ruling is explicit, not so it is a candidate
- **What:** the best category-(ii) implementation in existence. 50k★, v5.4.0 today, daily merges by its founder-CEO, and now moving toward local-first itself.
- **Why it does *not* survive:** the licence text forbids production use without a key; forbids interfering with key enforcement; forbids relicensing; **describes technical measures that detect deployment environments and transmit usage data to tldraw**; and requires the watermark stay "visible, unobscured, and unmodified." Against a sovereign, self-hosted, possibly-forked vessel with no mandatory server, that is not friction — it is the enclosure Orion Reed names, written into a clause.
- **Licence:** proprietary source-available (`NOASSERTION`).
- **Aliveness:** the best in the field, which is what makes it a trap rather than merely a wrong answer.
- **What would qualify it:** nothing we can do. Only tldraw relicensing, or an agreement whose terms permit fork and self-host with no telemetry. `!` Corollary: **`pvh/automerge-tldraw` is not a path** — 15 months stale, no tests, and two major versions behind.

---

### The through-line

`?` The field split cleanly. Every rasterising engine — Pixi, Konva, Fabric, CanvasKit, Excalidraw — is disqualified by the same mechanism your Flutter measurements already caught, and several convict themselves in their own documentation. Of the DOM-hosting family, exactly one library is simultaneously **right-family, permissively licensed, and healthy**: xyflow. The most *architecturally* aligned artifact, folkjs, is MIT and Automerge-native and **cooling**. The most *institutionally* aligned people, Ink & Switch, are thriving, funded through 2028, running our CRDT — and have **moved off canvas onto version control for spatial data**.

`_` The hidden shape: there is no maintained, permissively-licensed infinite-canvas *engine* built for hosting arbitrary live DOM. tldraw built it and closed it; folkjs built it and paused it. `0` I say plainly that the field has not settled here — which means the honest options are **adapt a graph library** (1) or **build the layer ourselves from published patterns** (2), and those two are not really rivals: option 1 is how we ship this year, option 2 is what DreamDOM was always going to become.

